investigate whether dependence on these two sets of token positions differs with the narrowness of generalization.

To identify representations that contribute to the learned behavior, we use reverse activation patching on these tokens (Zhao et al., 2026b). During prefill, we replace the fine-tuned model's residual-stream activations at a single layer with those of the base model before fine-tuning, using the same input. We patch either the prefix positions, comprising the system prompt and user-turn header, or the postfix positions, comprising the assistant-header tokens. A reduction in the behavior rate indicates that the patched representations contribute to its expression. We sweep all layers and evaluate each model under its training prompt.

Figure 4 shows that patching has its largest effects in the middle layers, but the relative importance of prefix and postfix positions varies across models. For goblin mentions, prefix patching substantially reduces the behavior in models with narrow generalization, whereas postfix patching has little effect. The broadly generalizing helpful-assistant model exhibits the opposite pattern. For reward hacking, postfix patching has larger effects for most models, while prefix patching becomes more influential in models with narrower generalization. These observations suggest a relationship between generalization narrowness and the token positions on which the learned behavior depends.

To examine this relationship systematically, we compare each model's generalization narrowness with the fraction of the learned effect removed by patching within layers 11-19 (Figure 5). Across both datasets, greater narrowness is associated with larger effects of prefix patching and smaller effects of postfix patching. This analysis supports the pattern suggested by the layer-wise results: narrower generalization is associated with greater dependence on prefix representations, while broader generalization is associated with greater dependence on postfix representations.

\section*{5 Interventions to modulate generalization narrowness}

In this section, we provide causal interventions that support our persona hierarchy model. We first show that modifying the default persona can broaden the generalization of subsequent finetuning under other prefixes, accompanied by reduced distance between their persona vector and the default (Section 5.1). We then deliberately align responses under a training prefix with those under the default prefix on separate web data, reducing persona distance and broadening generalization in subsequent fine-tuning (Section 5.2). These experiments provide intervention evidence supporting the relationship observed in Section 4.

\subsection*{5.1 Training history can broaden contextual generalization}

Two-stage training. We show that fine-tuning a model on one task under the default prefix \(s _ { \emptyset }\) without a system prompt can unexpectedly broaden the contextual generalization of subsequent fine-tuning under other prefix. Specifically, we train in two stages: (1) we first train the model on one dataset under the default prefix \(s _ { \emptyset }\); (2) we then continue to train the checkpoint on another dataset under a different prefix. Such sequential fine-tuning is common in practice. Post-training runs in multiple stages (Olmo et al., 2025; Microsoft AI Team, 2026), some of which train specific
